<td id="S1.T1.11.1.11.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem10" title="Proposition 4.10. ‣ Fine-Tuning with an Auxiliary Few-Shot Loss Improves Few-Shot Performance at the Expense of Generalization. ‣ 4.4 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot and Auxiliary Few-Shot Losses ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.10</span></a></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our main findings are summarized below and in Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fine-tuning all attention parameters to minimize the zero-shot loss can substantially degrade few-shot performance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Restricting fine-tuning to the value matrix while freezing the remaining attention parameters mitigates this degradation and preserves few-shot performance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fine-tuning with the zero-shot loss and an auxiliary few-shot loss further improves few-shot performance on the target task, but may reduce few-shot performance on tasks that differ from those used during fine-tuning.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Understanding In-context Learning of Transformers.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are studies that explain why Transformer models exhibit in-context learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Garg et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Min et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Akyürek et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dai et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mahankali et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin and Lee, 2024</a>)</cite>. One prominent line of work interprets in-context learning as implicit Bayesian inference over latent task variables <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Complementary work provides explanations by showing that simplified Transformer architectures can implement learning algorithms from the prompt: <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Von Oswald et al. (2023)</a></cite> prove that a linear self-attention layer is equivalent to a step of gradient descent, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al. (2023)</a></cite> further show that linear Transformers trained on regression tasks learn to implement preconditioned gradient descent for in-context learning. Motivated by this line of work, we adopt a theoretical formulation of Transformer models that assumes the pretrained model exhibits in-context learning capability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fine-Tuning Each Projection Matrix in Transformers.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work has studied matrix-wise fine-tuning of attention by updating the query, key, and value matrices separately, and finds that fine-tuning the value matrix often yields better performance than tuning the key or query matrices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. For example, Table 5 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2022)</a></cite> shows that low-rank adaptation on the value matrix achieves stronger performance on WikiSQL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2017</a>)</cite> than adapting the query or key matrices, while attaining performance comparable to that of fine-tuning all matrices under the two-standard-error criterion. However, these studies primarily evaluate performance on the target-task objective and do not assess whether fine-tuning degrades a pretrained model’s intrinsic capabilities, such as in-context learning. In our work, we show that value-matrix fine-tuning can achieve competitive target-task performance while preserving in-context learning capability, thereby maintaining general-purpose few-shot performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Problem Setup</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by clarifying notation.
Let <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbf{0}}_{d}:=[0\cdots 0]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{0}}_{d}:=[0\cdots 0]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbf{1}}_{d}:=[1\cdots 1]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{1}}_{d}:=[1\cdots 1]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the vector dimension. Let <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{I}}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{I}}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times d</annotation></semantics></math> identity matrix. For <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>, define <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[n]:=\{1,2,\cdots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[n]:=\{1,2,\cdots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\diag(a_{i})_{i\in[d]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\diag(a_{i})_{i\in[d]}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the diagonal matrix with <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th diagonal entry <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. We use block concatenation; for instance, <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\mathbf{Z}}\ {\mathbf{z}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\mathbf{Z}}\ {\mathbf{z}}]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the matrix obtained by appending the column vector <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the right of <math id="S3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, <math id="S3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\mathbf{Z}}]_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\mathbf{Z}}]_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\mathbf{z}}]_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\mathbf{z}}]_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>-th entry of <math id="S3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="S3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th entry of <math id="S3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we compare various Transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017b</a>)</cite> in terms of their test errors in zero-shot and few-shot settings. Following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Von Oswald et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mahankali et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim and Suzuki, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gozeten et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee, 2025</a>)</cite>, we focus on linear Transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Katharopoulos et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schlag et al., 2021</a>)</cite> for linear regression tasks. We formalize the regression tasks, data distribution, Transformer architecture, evaluation metrics, and training objectives as follows.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Regression Tasks and Data Distribution.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider linear regression tasks specified by a task vector <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where a <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional input <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped to a response <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a task <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, data points <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\mathbf{x}},y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbf{x}},y)</annotation></semantics></math> are generated independently as <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\sim\mathcal{N}({\mathbf{0}}_{d},{\mathbf{\Sigma}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\sim\mathcal{N}({\mathbf{0}}_{d},{\mathbf{\Sigma}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y={\mathbf{\theta}}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}+e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y={\mathbf{\theta}}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}+e</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. The covariance matrix <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\Sigma}}:={\mathbf{U}}{\mathbf{\Lambda}}{\mathbf{U}}^{\top}\in\mathbb{S}_{++}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>𝕊</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>+</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\Sigma}}:={\mathbf{U}}{\mathbf{\Lambda}}{\mathbf{U}}^{\top}\in\mathbb{S}_{++}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive definite, where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐔</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is orthogonal and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\Lambda}}=\diag(\lambda_{i})_{i\in[d]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\Lambda}}=\diag(\lambda_{i})_{i\in[d]}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}\geq\cdots\geq\lambda_{d}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≥</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}\geq\cdots\geq\lambda_{d}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a given task <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample a dataset <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{({\mathbf{x}}_{i},y_{i})\}_{i\in[n+1]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{({\mathbf{x}}_{i},y_{i})\}_{i\in[n+1]}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{i}:=\begin{bmatrix}1&{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{\top}&amp;y_{i}\end{bmatrix}^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{i}:=\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{\top}&amp;y_{i}\end{bmatrix}^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+2}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{n+1}:=\begin{bmatrix}1&{\mathbf{x}}_{n+1}^{\top}&amp;0\end{bmatrix}^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{n+1}:=\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;{\mathbf{x}}_{n+1}^{\top}&amp;0\end{bmatrix}^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+2}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose response entry is masked to zero. We then define the prompt matrix as</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{bmatrix}{\mathbf{Z}}_{[n]}&{\mathbf{z}}_{n+1}\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;\cdots&amp;1\\
{\mathbf{x}}_{1}&amp;\cdots&{\mathbf{x}}_{n}\\
y_{1}&amp;\cdots&amp;y_{n}\end{bmatrix}&amp;\begin{bmatrix}1\\
{\mathbf{x}}_{n+1}\\
0\end{bmatrix}\end{bmatrix}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{bmatrix}{\mathbf{Z}}_{[n]}&amp;{\mathbf{z}}_{n+1}\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;\cdots&amp;1\\
{\mathbf{x}}_{1}&amp;\cdots&amp;{\mathbf{x}}_{n}\\
y_{1}&amp;\cdots&amp;y_{n}\end{bmatrix}&amp;\begin{bmatrix}1\\
{\mathbf{x}}_{n+1}\\
0\end{bmatrix}\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbf{Z}}_{[n]}:=[{\mathbf{z}}_{1}\cdots{\mathbf{z}}_{n}]\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+2)\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Z}}_{[n]}:=[{\mathbf{z}}_{1}\cdots{\mathbf{z}}_{n}]\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+2)\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> stacks the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> context examples.
Hereafter, we omit the subscript <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> for notational simplicity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Transformer Architecture.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To model the regression task, we consider a linear self-attention layer that replaces the softmax function in standard self-attention with a linear function. For a prompt <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{ncol}({\mathbf{Z}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ncol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{ncol}({\mathbf{Z}})</annotation></semantics></math> columns, the linear attention model <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f({\mathbf{Z}};{\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}}):={\mathbf{Z}}+\tfrac{1}{\mathrm{ncol}({\mathbf{Z}})}\cdot{\mathbf{V}}{\mathbf{Z}}{\mathbf{Z}}^{\top}{\mathbf{Q}}{\mathbf{Z}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐙</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>ncol</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>𝐕𝐙𝐙</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐐𝐙</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f({\mathbf{Z}};{\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}}):={\mathbf{Z}}+\tfrac{1}{\mathrm{ncol}({\mathbf{Z}})}\cdot{\mathbf{V}}{\mathbf{Z}}{\mathbf{Z}}^{\top}{\mathbf{Q}}{\mathbf{Z}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathbf{V}}:={\begin{bmatrix}0&{\mathbf{0}}_{d}^{\top}&amp;0\\
{\mathbf{0}}_{d}&{\mathbf{V}}_{11}&{\mathbf{v}}_{12}\\
0&{\mathbf{v}}_{21}^{\top}&amp;v_{22}\end{bmatrix}}\!,\quad{\mathbf{Q}}:={\begin{bmatrix}q&{\mathbf{0}}_{d}^{\top}&amp;0\\
{\mathbf{0}}_{d}&{\mathbf{Q}}_{11}&{\mathbf{q}}_{12}\\
0&{\mathbf{q}}_{21}^{\top}&amp;q_{22}\end{bmatrix}}\!," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>d</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>d</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>21</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>22</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>q</mi></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>d</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>d</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mn>11</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mn>12</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐪</mi><mn>21</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>22</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathbf{V}}:={\begin{bmatrix}0&amp;{\mathbf{0}}_{d}^{\top}&amp;0\\
{\mathbf{0}}_{d}&amp;{\mathbf{V}}_{11}&amp;{\mathbf{v}}_{12}\\
0&amp;{\mathbf{v}}_{21}^{\top}&amp;v_{22}\end{bmatrix}}\!,\quad{\mathbf{Q}}:={\begin{bmatrix}q&amp;{\mathbf{0}}_{d}^{\top}&amp;0\\
{\mathbf{0}}_{d}&amp;{\mathbf{Q}}_{11}&amp;{\mathbf{q}}_{12}\\
0&amp;{\mathbf{q}}_{21}^{\top}&amp;q_{22}\end{bmatrix}}\!,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+2)\times(d+2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}}\in\mathbb{R}^{(d+2)\times(d+2)}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the value projection matrix and the merged query–key projection matrix, respectively. Here <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{V}}_{11},{\mathbf{Q}}_{11}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐕</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{V}}_{11},{\mathbf{Q}}_{11}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> are matrices, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{v}}_{12},{\mathbf{v}}_{21},{\mathbf{q}}_{12},{\mathbf{q}}_{21}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{v}}_{12},{\mathbf{v}}_{21},{\mathbf{q}}_{12},{\mathbf{q}}_{21}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are vectors, and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{22},q,q_{22}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>22</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{22},q,q_{22}</annotation></semantics></math> are scalars. Our formulation generalizes existing linear attention models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>; in particular, setting <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Transformer Architecture. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) recovers their model. Moreover, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al. (2024)</a></cite> show that linear attention models trained on regression tasks serve as a proxy for studying the optimization of Transformers, exhibiting behavior similar to standard Transformers in practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the model <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Transformer Architecture. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), we take the last entry of its output as the prediction of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\!\!\!\hat{y}_{\mathrm{FS}}({\mathbf{Z}}_{[n]},{\mathbf{x}};{\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>FS</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\!\!\!\hat{y}_{\mathrm{FS}}({\mathbf{Z}}_{[n]},{\mathbf{x}};{\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\left[f\!\left(\begin{bmatrix}{\mathbf{Z}}_{[n]}\!\!&amp;\!\!{\mathbf{z}}\end{bmatrix}\!\!;{\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}}\right)\right]_{d+2,n+1}\!," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.427em;" width="0.427em"><mi>f</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mi>𝐳</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\left[f\!\left(\begin{bmatrix}{\mathbf{Z}}_{[n]}\!\!&amp;\!\!{\mathbf{z}}\end{bmatrix}\!\!;{\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}}\right)\right]_{d+2,n+1}\!,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{y}_{\mathrm{ZS}}({\mathbf{x}};{\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ZS</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{y}_{\mathrm{ZS}}({\mathbf{x}};{\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\left[f\left({\mathbf{z}};{\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}}\right)\right]_{d+2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\left[f\left({\mathbf{z}};{\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}}\right)\right]_{d+2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{\mathrm{FS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>FS</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{\mathrm{FS}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Transformer Architecture. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) denotes the few-shot prediction from a prompt containing <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> examples, whereas <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{\mathrm{ZS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ZS</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{\mathrm{ZS}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ Transformer Architecture. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) denotes the zero-shot prediction from a prompt containing no examples, i.e., using only <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation Metric and Training Objective.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate model performance under zero-shot and few-shot settings using the following test error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Test Error)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> and a task <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-shot test error on the task <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{E}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}}):=\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{Z}}_{[n]},\,{\mathbf{x}},\,y\mid{\mathbf{\theta}}}\!\left[\big(\hat{y}_{\mathrm{FS}}({\mathbf{Z}}_{[n]},{\mathbf{x}};{\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}})\!-\!y\big)^{2}\right]\!," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>FS</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mpadded style="width:0.108em;" width="0.108em"><mo>]</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{E}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}}):=\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{Z}}_{[n]},\,{\mathbf{x}},\,y\mid{\mathbf{\theta}}}\!\left[\big(\hat{y}_{\mathrm{FS}}({\mathbf{Z}}_{[n]},{\mathbf{x}};{\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}})\!-\!y\big)^{2}\right]\!,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and the zero-shot test error on the task <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{E}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};0,{\mathbf{\theta}}):=\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},\,y\mid{\mathbf{\theta}}}\left[\big(\hat{y}_{\mathrm{ZS}}({\mathbf{x}};{\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}})-y\big)^{2}\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ZS</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{E}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};0,{\mathbf{\theta}}):=\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{x}},\,y\mid{\mathbf{\theta}}}\left[\big(\hat{y}_{\mathrm{ZS}}({\mathbf{x}};{\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}})-y\big)^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by prior work demonstrating that Transformers exhibit in-context learning behavior on linear regression tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gozeten et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we model pretraining by minimizing the expected <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-shot test error averaged over tasks. Concretely, let <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the number of context examples used during pretraining, which may differ from the test-time number of examples <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Although pretraining may involve various context lengths <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, we fix <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> for analytical purposes. We define the pretraining loss as</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}}):=\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{\theta}}}\big[\mathcal{E}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};m,{\mathbf{\theta}})\big]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}}):=\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{\theta}}}\big[\mathcal{E}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};m,{\mathbf{\theta}})\big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Starting from the model pretrained via (<a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Evaluation Metric and Training Objective. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), we consider fine-tuning to improve zero-shot performance on the target task vector <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, by minimizing the zero-shot prompt loss</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ZS}}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}):=\mathcal{E}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};0,{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ZS</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ZS}}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}):=\mathcal{E}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};0,{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We characterize the resulting models under these training objectives and analyze the zero-shot and few-shot test errors defined in Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 3.1 (Test Error). ‣ Evaluation Metric and Training Objective. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we theoretically analyze the linear attention model <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Transformer Architecture. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) under different training regimes.
Starting from a pretrained model, we consider fine-tuning variants that improve zero-shot performance on a target task <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> by minimizing the zero-shot prompt loss in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Evaluation Metric and Training Objective. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).
These variants differ in which attention parameters are updated and whether an auxiliary few-shot prompt loss is included.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Pretraining</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, we consider a linear attention model pretrained using the loss <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Evaluation Metric and Training Objective. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). During pretraining, the training data are generated from task vectors <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> sampled from <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}({\mathbf{0}}_{d},{\mathbf{I}}_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}({\mathbf{0}}_{d},{\mathbf{I}}_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>. In the zero-shot setting, where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is predicted directly from <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> without observing any additional examples, a single input <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> does not provide information about the underlying task vector <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, the Bayesian optimal predictor under squared loss relies on the prior mean <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[{\mathbf{\theta}}]={\mathbf{0}}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[{\mathbf{\theta}}]={\mathbf{0}}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding zero prediction for all <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. By contrast, when few-shot examples are provided, the model can implicitly infer the shared task vector and adapt its predictions accordingly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A linear attention model pretrained by minimizing the loss <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Evaluation Metric and Training Objective. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) exhibits this behavior, which is formalized by the following corollaries. Proofs of all statements in this section are provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Proofs for Optimal Parameters ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a> and <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Proofs for Test Errors ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Optimal Parameters of Pretrained Models; Corollary of Theorem 1 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al. (2023)</a></cite>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Transformer Architecture. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) satisfy <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}_{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}_{11}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive definite.
Consider the loss <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Evaluation Metric and Training Objective. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) with context length <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for sufficiently large <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, the following parameters globally minimize the loss:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S4.E10.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:310.5pt;height:46.3pt;vertical-align:-19.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(45.9pt,-6.8pt) scale(1.42013277600334,1.42013277600334) ;">
<p id="S4.E10.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.E10.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}\!\!=\!\!\begin{bmatrix}\cdot\;\;\cdot\!\!&amp;\!\!\cdot\\
\cdot\;\;\cdot\!\!&amp;\!\!\cdot\\
\cdot\;\;\cdot\!\!&amp;\!\!\!\tfrac{m}{m+1+d}\end{bmatrix}\!\!,\;\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}\!\!=\!\!\begin{bmatrix}\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;\!\!\!{\mathbf{U}}\diag\!\Big(\tfrac{m+1+d}{(m+1)\lambda_{i}+\tr({\mathbf{\Sigma}})}\Big)_{i\in[d]}\!\!\!\!\!\!{\mathbf{U}}^{\top}\!\!\!&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\end{bmatrix}\!\!,\!\!\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mpadded style="width:0.716em;" width="0.716em"><mo>=</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>⋅</mo><mo lspace="0.560em">⁣</mo><mo>⋅</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>⋅</mo><mo lspace="0.560em">⁣</mo><mo>⋅</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>⋅</mo><mo lspace="0.560em">⁣</mo><mo>⋅</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mpadded style="width:0.716em;" width="0.716em"><mo>=</mo></mpadded><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.736em;" width="1.736em"><mi>diag</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}\!\!=\!\!\begin{bmatrix}\cdot\;\;\cdot\!\!&amp;\!\!\cdot\\
\cdot\;\;\cdot\!\!&amp;\!\!\cdot\\
\cdot\;\;\cdot\!\!&amp;\!\!\!\tfrac{m}{m+1+d}\end{bmatrix}\!\!,\;\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}\!\!=\!\!\begin{bmatrix}\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;\!\!\!{\mathbf{U}}\diag\!\Big(\tfrac{m+1+d}{(m+1)\lambda_{i}+\tr({\mathbf{\Sigma}})}\Big)_{i\in[d]}\!\!\!\!\!\!{\mathbf{U}}^{\top}\!\!\!&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\end{bmatrix}\!\!,\!\!\!</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All unspecified blocks (denoted by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>) are zero.
Moreover, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{U}}\diag\!\Big(\tfrac{m+1+d}{(m+1)\lambda_{i}+\tr({\mathbf{\Sigma}})}\Big)_{i\in[d]}{\mathbf{U}}^{\top}\to{\mathbf{\Sigma}}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.736em;" width="1.736em"><mi>diag</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{U}}\diag\!\Big(\tfrac{m+1+d}{(m+1)\lambda_{i}+\tr({\mathbf{\Sigma}})}\Big)_{i\in[d]}{\mathbf{U}}^{\top}\to{\mathbf{\Sigma}}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Test Error of Pretrained Models)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the parameters in (<a href="#S4.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ Corollary 4.1 (Optimal Parameters of Pretrained Models; Corollary of Theorem 1 in ( ) ). ‣ 4.1 Pretraining ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) which minimize the loss <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Evaluation Metric and Training Objective. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). Then, for any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the following holds:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma^{2}+{\mathbf{\theta}}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}=\mathcal{E}(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}},\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};0,{\mathbf{\theta}})\geq\mathcal{E}(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}},\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma^{2}+{\mathbf{\theta}}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}=\mathcal{E}(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}},\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};0,{\mathbf{\theta}})\geq\mathcal{E}(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}},\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\sum_{i\in[d]}a_{i}^{2}\cdot\frac{\lambda_{1}\left\lVert{\mathbf{\theta}}\right\rVert_{2}^{2}+\sigma^{2}}{a_{d}(2-a_{d})\cdot\lambda_{1}\left\lVert{\mathbf{\theta}}\right\rVert_{2}^{2}}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\sum_{i\in[d]}a_{i}^{2}\cdot\frac{\lambda_{1}\left\lVert{\mathbf{\theta}}\right\rVert_{2}^{2}+\sigma^{2}}{a_{d}(2-a_{d})\cdot\lambda_{1}\left\lVert{\mathbf{\theta}}\right\rVert_{2}^{2}}-1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}:=\frac{m\lambda_{i}}{(m+1)\lambda_{i}+\tr({\mathbf{\Sigma}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}:=\frac{m\lambda_{i}}{(m+1)\lambda_{i}+\tr({\mathbf{\Sigma}})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-shot error is monotone decreasing in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus the minimum error is</span></p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S4.E13.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:17.7pt;vertical-align:-5.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(55.5pt,-2.9pt) scale(1.4744827178732,1.4744827178732) ;">
<p id="S4.E13.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.E13.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{n\to\infty}\mathcal{E}(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}},\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}})=\sigma^{2}+{\mathbf{\theta}}^{\top}{\mathbf{U}}\diag((a_{i}\!-\!1)^{2}\lambda_{i})_{i\in[d]}{\mathbf{U}}^{\top}{\mathbf{\theta}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐔</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{n\to\infty}\mathcal{E}(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}},\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}})=\sigma^{2}+{\mathbf{\theta}}^{\top}{\mathbf{U}}\diag((a_{i}\!-\!1)^{2}\lambda_{i})_{i\in[d]}{\mathbf{U}}^{\top}{\mathbf{\theta}},</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which converges to <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}\to 1</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Few-Shot Performance Outperforms Zero-Shot Only with Sufficient Demonstrations.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Corollary 4.2 (Test Error of Pretrained Models). ‣ 4.1 Pretraining ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> shows that the few-shot performance improves monotonically as the number of shots <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> increases, but can be worse than the zero-shot performance for small <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate this phenomenon, consider an isotropic setting in which inputs are sampled as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}\sim\mathcal{N}({\mathbf{0}}_{d},\lambda{\mathbf{I}}_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}\sim\mathcal{N}({\mathbf{0}}_{d},\lambda{\mathbf{I}}_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, the condition in (<a href="#S4.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Corollary 4.2 (Test Error of Pretrained Models). ‣ 4.1 Pretraining ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) under which the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-shot performance outperforms the zero-shot reduces to</p>
<table id="S4.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n\geq\frac{da}{2-a}\Bigl(1+\frac{\sigma^{2}}{\lambda\|{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\|_{2}^{2}}\Bigr)-1,\quad a:=\frac{m}{m+1+d}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>:=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n\geq\frac{da}{2-a}\Bigl(1+\frac{\sigma^{2}}{\lambda\|{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\|_{2}^{2}}\Bigr)-1,\quad a:=\frac{m}{m+1+d}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This condition is both necessary and sufficient, see Corollary <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem15" title="Corollary A.15. ‣ A.4 Proofs for Test Errors ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.15</span></a>. When <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is below this threshold, few-shot performance is strictly worse than the zero-shot baseline, in particular for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\{1,\cdots,d-2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\{1,\cdots,d-2\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.23197v1/optimal_pretrained.svg" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/238;" width="476" height="238"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<math id="S4.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-shot test error on the target task <math id="S4.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of the pretrained model using the theory-derived parameters in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Corollary 4.1 (Optimal Parameters of Pretrained Models; Corollary of Theorem 1 in ( ) ). ‣ 4.1 Pretraining ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>. Each curve corresponds to a model pretrained with context length <math id="S4.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1000</annotation></semantics></math> and a different input dimension <math id="S4.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. We set <math id="S4.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, under which the condition in (<a href="#S4.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ Few-Shot Performance Outperforms Zero-Shot Only with Sufficient Demonstrations. ‣ 4.1 Pretraining ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) predicts that the <math id="S4.F1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-shot performance is worse than zero-shot performance for <math id="S4.F1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> ranging from <math id="S4.F1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.F1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Few-Shot Performance Outperforms Zero-Shot Only with Sufficient Demonstrations. ‣ 4.1 Pretraining ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> visualizes this effect for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1000</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, where the threshold in (<a href="#S4.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ Few-Shot Performance Outperforms Zero-Shot Only with Sufficient Demonstrations. ‣ 4.1 Pretraining ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) is approximately <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-1</annotation></semantics></math>. This behavior arises from pretraining at a fixed and sufficiently large context length <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, for which <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\to 1</annotation></semantics></math>. As a result, the same effect can appear even when multiple context lengths are used during pretraining, but may not persist if smaller context lengths are included. This phenomenon should not be confused with double descent or early ascent, as each curve corresponds to a final pretrained model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The pretrained model specified by (<a href="#S4.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ Corollary 4.1 (Optimal Parameters of Pretrained Models; Corollary of Theorem 1 in ( ) ). ‣ 4.1 Pretraining ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) exhibits two limitations. First, achieving improved performance requires a sufficiently large number of examples <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Second, in the zero-shot setting, the model predicts under the prior mean assumption <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[{\mathbf{\theta}}]={\mathbf{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[{\mathbf{\theta}}]={\mathbf{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and therefore produces the same prediction regardless of the target task vector <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. This naturally motivates fine-tuning as a mechanism for specializing the model to a given target task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For simplicity, unless stated otherwise, we assume the model is pretrained in the limit <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the middle block <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}_{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>11</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}_{11}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ Corollary 4.1 (Optimal Parameters of Pretrained Models; Corollary of Theorem 1 in ( ) ). ‣ 4.1 Pretraining ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) converges to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\Sigma}}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\Sigma}}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Fine-Tuning All Parameters with Zero-Shot Loss</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now study fine-tuned linear attention models, where all parameters <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> are optimized to improve zero-shot performance on the target task <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Fine-tuning starts from the pretrained model in (<a href="#S4.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ Corollary 4.1 (Optimal Parameters of Pretrained Models; Corollary of Theorem 1 in ( ) ). ‣ 4.1 Pretraining ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) and minimizes the zero-shot loss <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ZS}}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ZS</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ZS}}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Evaluation Metric and Training Objective. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).
The following results characterize the optimal parameters and the test error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Optimal Parameters of Fully Fine-Tuned Models)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a target task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the zero-shot prompt loss <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ZS}}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ZS</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ZS}}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Evaluation Metric and Training Objective. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).
Then, for any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the following parameters globally minimize the loss:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w)=\begin{bmatrix}\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;w{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}&amp;1\end{bmatrix},\quad\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}(w)=\begin{bmatrix}1/w&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\end{bmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w)=\begin{bmatrix}\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;w{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}&amp;1\end{bmatrix},\quad\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}(w)=\begin{bmatrix}1/w&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\end{bmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Test Error of Fully Fine-Tuned Models)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a target task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the family of parameters <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}(w)\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}(w)\big)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ Theorem 4.3 (Optimal Parameters of Fully Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.2 Fine-Tuning All Parameters with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>). Then, for any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the following inequality holds:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma^{2}=\mathcal{E}(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}(w);0,{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})&lt;\mathcal{E}(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}(w);n,{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma^{2}=\mathcal{E}(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}(w);0,{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})&lt;\mathcal{E}(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}(w);n,{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, for any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the test errors are given by</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{E}(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}(w);0,{\mathbf{\theta}})=\sigma^{2}+({\mathbf{\theta}}-{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}({\mathbf{\theta}}-{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{E}(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}(w);0,{\mathbf{\theta}})=\sigma^{2}+({\mathbf{\theta}}-{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}({\mathbf{\theta}}-{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\mathcal{E}(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}(w);n,{\mathbf{\theta}})=\sigma^{2}+{\mathbf{\theta}}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\mathcal{E}(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}(w);n,{\mathbf{\theta}})=\sigma^{2}+{\mathbf{\theta}}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fine-Tuning All Parameters Degrades Few-Shot Performance Even on the Target Task.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Corollary 4.4 (Test Error of Fully Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.2 Fine-Tuning All Parameters with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> reveals a trade-off when all parameters are fine-tuned to optimize zero-shot performance. While the resulting model attains the minimum test error <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in the zero-shot setting, this gain comes at the cost of degraded few-shot performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As the number of shots <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the few-shot test error on a task <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> converges to
<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}+{\mathbf{\theta}}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}+{\mathbf{\theta}}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be substantially larger than the zero-shot error.
Notably, this degradation persists even on the target task <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, although the model is optimal for zero-shot performance on <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.23197v1/optimal_zs_finetune.svg" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/238;" width="476" height="238"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<math id="S4.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-shot test error on the target task <math id="S4.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of the fully fine-tuned model using the theory-derived parameters in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3 (Optimal Parameters of Fully Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.2 Fine-Tuning All Parameters with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>. Each curve corresponds to a model with a different choice of the parameter <math id="S4.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, evaluated under <math id="S4.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. For all values of <math id="S4.F2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, the zero-shot error is <math id="S4.F2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas the few-shot error is larger than the zero-shot error and converges to <math id="S4.F2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.F2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Fine-Tuning All Parameters Degrades Few-Shot Performance Even on the Target Task. ‣ 4.2 Fine-Tuning All Parameters with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrates this phenomenon. Although minimizing the zero-shot loss admits a family of optimal solutions parameterized by a free parameter <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3 (Optimal Parameters of Fully Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.2 Fine-Tuning All Parameters with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, all such choices yield worse few-shot performance than zero-shot performance. In particular, for every <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the few-shot error exceeds the zero-shot error and converges to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, which equals <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> in the setting of Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Fine-Tuning All Parameters Degrades Few-Shot Performance Even on the Target Task. ‣ 4.2 Fine-Tuning All Parameters with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These results reveal a limitation of full fine-tuning. While it optimizes zero-shot performance on the target task <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, it degrades few-shot performance, making few-shot prediction strictly worse than zero-shot prediction even on the target task <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. This observation motivates alternative fine-tuning strategies that preserve effective few-shot performance, which we study next.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To improve zero-shot performance on the target task <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> while preserving few-shot performance, we revisit the structure of the optimal parameters derived in the previous subsections. A key insight from Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3 (Optimal Parameters of Fully Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.2 Fine-Tuning All Parameters with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> is that, under full fine-tuning, the target task vector <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> may be encoded in the value matrix through the parameter <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{v}}_{21}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{v}}_{21}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this sense, the value component of the attention mechanism provides a pathway to improved zero-shot performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize this by characterizing when zero-shot performance outperforms few-shot performance, and show that this comparison is governed by the value parameter <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{v}}_{21}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{v}}_{21}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Transformer Architecture. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) satisfy <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{22}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>22</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{22}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{q}}_{21}={\mathbf{0}}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{q}}_{21}={\mathbf{0}}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}_{11}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}_{11}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive definite. For any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the following inequality holds:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{E}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};0,{\mathbf{\theta}})\leq\mathcal{E}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{E}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};0,{\mathbf{\theta}})\leq\mathcal{E}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">if and only if</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left({\mathbf{v}}_{21}-{\mathbf{A}}^{-1}{\mathbf{B}}{\mathbf{\theta}}\right)^{\top}{\mathbf{A}}\left({\mathbf{v}}_{21}-{\mathbf{A}}^{-1}{\mathbf{B}}{\mathbf{\theta}}\right)\leq c," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left({\mathbf{v}}_{21}-{\mathbf{A}}^{-1}{\mathbf{B}}{\mathbf{\theta}}\right)^{\top}{\mathbf{A}}\left({\mathbf{v}}_{21}-{\mathbf{A}}^{-1}{\mathbf{B}}{\mathbf{\theta}}\right)\leq c,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c:={\mathbf{\theta}}^{\top}({\mathbf{B}}{\mathbf{A}}^{-1}{\mathbf{B}}+{\mathbf{C}}){\mathbf{\theta}}+(\tr({\mathbf{Q}}_{11}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{Q}}_{11}{\mathbf{\Sigma}})+q^{2})\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐁𝐀</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐁</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐂</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:={\mathbf{\theta}}^{\top}({\mathbf{B}}{\mathbf{A}}^{-1}{\mathbf{B}}+{\mathbf{C}}){\mathbf{\theta}}+(\tr({\mathbf{Q}}_{11}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{Q}}_{11}{\mathbf{\Sigma}})+q^{2})\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}},{\mathbf{B}},{\mathbf{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐂</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}},{\mathbf{B}},{\mathbf{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> are matrices determined by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}},{\mathbf{\Sigma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝚺</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}},{\mathbf{\Sigma}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 4.5. ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> makes the role of the value parameter explicit. While <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> depend on <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\Sigma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\Sigma}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and the task vector <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> at which the test error is evaluated, they are independent of <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{v}}_{21}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{v}}_{21}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, the inequality in (<a href="#S4.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ Theorem 4.5. ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) can be satisfied only through updates to <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{v}}_{21}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{v}}_{21}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, moving <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{v}}_{21}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{v}}_{21}</annotation></semantics></math> toward <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}^{-1}{\mathbf{B}}{\mathbf{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐀</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}^{-1}{\mathbf{B}}{\mathbf{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> makes the condition hold, which corresponds to a regime where zero-shot performance outperforms few-shot performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">At the same time, recall that few-shot performance in the pretrained model relies on the query-key matrix <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and in particular on the condition <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}_{11}={\mathbf{\Sigma}}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mn>11</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}_{11}={\mathbf{\Sigma}}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> in Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Corollary 4.1 (Optimal Parameters of Pretrained Models; Corollary of Theorem 1 in ( ) ). ‣ 4.1 Pretraining ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>. This observation suggests a fine-tuning strategy: rather than updating both <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we freeze <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> to preserve the mechanism enabling few-shot performance and fine-tune only the value matrix <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐕</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> to improve zero-shot performance. We refer to this strategy as <em id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">value-matrix fine-tuning</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem characterizes the models obtained by value-matrix fine-tuning under the zero-shot prompt loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Value-Matrix Fine-Tuned Models)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a target task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the zero-shot prompt loss <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ZS}}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ZS</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ZS}}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Evaluation Metric and Training Objective. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).
For any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, define</span></p>
<table id="S4.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w)=\begin{bmatrix}\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;\frac{1}{d+4}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}&amp;w\end{bmatrix}\!,\quad\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}=\begin{bmatrix}\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&{\mathbf{\Sigma}}^{-1}&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\end{bmatrix}\!." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mi>w</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi>𝚺</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>⋅</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w)=\begin{bmatrix}\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;\frac{1}{d+4}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}&amp;w\end{bmatrix}\!,\quad\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}=\begin{bmatrix}\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;{\mathbf{\Sigma}}^{-1}&amp;\cdot\\
\cdot&amp;\cdot&amp;\cdot\end{bmatrix}\!.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w)</annotation></semantics></math> minimizes the zero-shot loss for fixed <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</span></p>
<table id="S4.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w)\in\argmin_{{\mathbf{V}}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ZS}}({\mathbf{V}},\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>𝐕</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ZS</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w)\in\argmin_{{\mathbf{V}}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ZS}}({\mathbf{V}},\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the minimum value is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}+\frac{2}{d+4}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}+\frac{2}{d+4}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under this choice of parameters, for any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\mathcal{E}\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\mathcal{E}\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}}\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sigma^{2}+\big(\tfrac{1}{d+4}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}+(w-1){\mathbf{\theta}}\big)^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}\big(\tfrac{1}{d+4}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}+(w-1){\mathbf{\theta}}\big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sigma^{2}+\big(\tfrac{1}{d+4}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}+(w-1){\mathbf{\theta}}\big)^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}\big(\tfrac{1}{d+4}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}+(w-1){\mathbf{\theta}}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Under the constraint that <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> is frozen at the matrix <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}</annotation></semantics></math> learned during pretraining, the model cannot generally attain the irreducible minimum error <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in the zero-shot setting. Nevertheless, the resulting zero-shot error, <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}+\frac{2}{d+4}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}+\frac{2}{d+4}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, is close to <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for sufficiently large <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. More importantly, by preserving the pretrained query–key matrix, value-matrix fine-tuning maintains strong few-shot performance and can outperform full fine-tuning in the few-shot regime.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The parameter <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E21" title="Equation 21 ‣ Theorem 4.6 (Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Value-Matrix Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) does not affect zero-shot performance, so the zero-shot objective does not uniquely determine <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. Because fine-tuning starts from the pretrained parameters in (<a href="#S4.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ Corollary 4.1 (Optimal Parameters of Pretrained Models; Corollary of Theorem 1 in ( ) ). ‣ 4.1 Pretraining ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), it is natural to choose <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> to make the update as small as possible. Among all zero-shot optimal solutions <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w)</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 4.6 (Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Value-Matrix Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>, we select the one closest to the pretrained parameters. The following results formalize this choice of <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and characterize the resulting model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.7</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\Sigma}}={\mathbf{I}}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\Sigma}}={\mathbf{I}}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the parameters in (<a href="#S4.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ Corollary 4.1 (Optimal Parameters of Pretrained Models; Corollary of Theorem 1 in ( ) ). ‣ 4.1 Pretraining ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) which minimize the loss <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Evaluation Metric and Training Objective. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).
Given a target task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E21" title="Equation 21 ‣ Theorem 4.6 (Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Value-Matrix Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>).
Then</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}\left(\tfrac{m}{m+1+d}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}\left(\tfrac{m}{m+1+d}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\argmin\Big\{\big\|{\mathbf{V}}\!-\!\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}\big\|_{\mathrm{F}}^{2}\Big|{\mathbf{V}}\in\argmin_{{\mathbf{V}}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ZS}}({\mathbf{V}},\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})\Big\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.600em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>𝐕</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>ZS</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\argmin\Big\{\big\|{\mathbf{V}}\!-\!\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}\big\|_{\mathrm{F}}^{2}\Big|{\mathbf{V}}\in\argmin_{{\mathbf{V}}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ZS}}({\mathbf{V}},\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})\Big\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert\cdot\right\rVert_{\mathrm{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert\cdot\right\rVert_{\mathrm{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Frobenius norm.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n</annotation></semantics></math>, then for sufficiently large <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+d</annotation></semantics></math>, the choice <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=\frac{m}{m+1+d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=\frac{m}{m+1+d}</annotation></semantics></math> closely approximates the task-averaged optimum <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\star}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\star}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>, defined in Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem8" title="Corollary 4.8 (Task-Averaged Optimal 𝑤). ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> below.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.8</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Task-Averaged Optimal <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a target task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the family of parameters <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E21" title="Equation 21 ‣ Theorem 4.6 (Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Value-Matrix Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>).
For any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the few-shot test error averaged over tasks
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}\sim\mathcal{N}({\mathbf{0}}_{d},{\mathbf{I}}_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}\sim\mathcal{N}({\mathbf{0}}_{d},{\mathbf{I}}_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> is minimized at</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w^{\star}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w^{\star}(n)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\argmin_{w}\,\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{\theta}}}\!\left[\mathcal{E}\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}}\big)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\argmin_{w}\,\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{{\mathbf{\theta}}}\!\left[\mathcal{E}\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}}\big)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{(n+1)\tr({\mathbf{\Sigma}})}{(n+1+d)\tr({\mathbf{\Sigma}})+d\sigma^{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{(n+1)\tr({\mathbf{\Sigma}})}{(n+1+d)\tr({\mathbf{\Sigma}})+d\sigma^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\star}(n)\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\star}(n)\to 1</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, for any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{n\to\infty}\mathcal{E}\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(1),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}}\big)=\sigma^{2}+\frac{1}{(d+4)^{2}}\,{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{n\to\infty}\mathcal{E}\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(1),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}}\big)=\sigma^{2}+\frac{1}{(d+4)^{2}}\,{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Value-Matrix Fine-Tuning Improves Zero-Shot Performance While Preserving Few-Shot Performance.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem7" title="Proposition 4.7. ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a> fixes the free parameter <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> using a minimal-update rule: among all value-matrix fine-tuning solutions <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w)</annotation></semantics></math> that minimize the zero-shot loss, it chooses the one closest to the pretrained matrix <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem8" title="Corollary 4.8 (Task-Averaged Optimal 𝑤). ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> provides a complementary characterization of this choice from the few-shot perspective. It shows that, when tasks are drawn from <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}\sim\mathcal{N}({\mathbf{0}}_{d},{\mathbf{I}}_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mn>𝟎</mn><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}\sim\mathcal{N}({\mathbf{0}}_{d},{\mathbf{I}}_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, the task-averaged few-shot test error admits a unique minimizer <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\star}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\star}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E27" title="Equation 27 ‣ Corollary 4.8 (Task-Averaged Optimal 𝑤). ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>). In the limit as <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\star}(n)\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\star}(n)\to 1</annotation></semantics></math>, implying that the asymptotic few-shot error becomes identical for all tasks <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, when <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+d</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficiently large, the choice <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=\tfrac{m}{m+1+d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=\tfrac{m}{m+1+d}</annotation></semantics></math> from Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem7" title="Proposition 4.7. ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a> closely approximates <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\star}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\star}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies that the resulting value-matrix fine-tuned model preserves few-shot performance uniformly over tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Fine-Tuning Value Matrix            with Zero-Shot and Auxiliary Few-Shot Losses</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Value-matrix fine-tuning with the minimal-update rule in Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem7" title="Proposition 4.7. ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a> improves zero-shot performance on the target task <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> while preserving few-shot performance across tasks. Without imposing the minimal-update rule, there exist multiple parameter choices that achieve the same zero-shot optimum. As shown in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 4.6 (Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Value-Matrix Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>, these solutions form a family <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w)</annotation></semantics></math> parameterized by a scalar <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and different choices of <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> lead to different few-shot behavior. This remaining degree of freedom enables further adaptation beyond zero-shot performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, few-shot performance is controlled by <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, and the optimal <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> depends on both the evaluation task <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the number of demonstrations <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> at test time. This suggests that value-matrix fine-tuning can be refined by selecting <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> to optimize <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-shot performance on a specific task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.4 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot and Auxiliary Few-Shot Losses ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> illustrates this dependence by plotting the <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-shot test error of models with parameters <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E21" title="Equation 21 ‣ Theorem 4.6 (Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Value-Matrix Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) for different values of <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each fixed <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an optimal choice of <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> that minimizes the <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-shot test error on the target task. The following theorem formalizes this observation by characterizing the task-wise optimal choice of <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of the evaluation task <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the number of demonstrations <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.23197v1/optimal_zs_finetune_v_only_with_c.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S4.F3.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Top) <math id="S4.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></span>-shot test error on the target task <math id="S4.F3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for the value-matrix fine-tuned model with the theory-derived parameters <math id="S4.F3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.330em" minsize="1.330em">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mo maxsize="1.330em" minsize="1.330em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 4.6 (Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Value-Matrix Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a>. Each curve corresponds to a different choice of <math id="S4.F3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. We use <math id="S4.F3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.F3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, for which the zero-shot test error equals <math id="S4.F3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.1\,(=\sigma^{2}+\tfrac{2}{d+4}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.1</mn><mspace style="width:0.559em;" width="0.559em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.1\,(=\sigma^{2}+\tfrac{2}{d+4}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> for all models.
<span id="S4.F3.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Bottom) </span>Optimal <math id="S4.F3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\star}(n;{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\star}(n;{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> that minimizes the <math id="S4.F3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-shot test error on the target task <math id="S4.F3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The optimal value increases monotonically with <math id="S4.F3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and converges to <math id="S4.F3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8/9\,(=\tfrac{d+3}{d+4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:0.559em;" width="0.559em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8/9\,(=\tfrac{d+3}{d+4})</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, the <math id="S4.F3.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math>-shot test error is minimized at <math id="S4.F3.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\approx 0.66" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.66</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\approx 0.66</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.23197v1/linear_tf_legend.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/13;" width="476" height="13"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.23197v1/linear_tf_pretrained.svg" id="S4.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/381;" width="476" height="381"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span>
<span id="S4.F4.sf1.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:207.0pt;">
<span id="S4.F4.sf1.5.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pretraining</span></span>
</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.23197v1/linear_tf_zs.svg" id="S4.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/381;" width="476" height="381"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span>
<span id="S4.F4.sf2.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:310.5pt;">
<span id="S4.F4.sf2.5.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fine-tuning all parameters</span></span>
<span id="S4.F4.sf2.5.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.5.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">with zero-shot loss</span></span>
</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.23197v1/linear_tf_zs_v.svg" id="S4.F4.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/381;" width="476" height="381"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span>
<span id="S4.F4.sf3.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:258.8pt;">
<span id="S4.F4.sf3.5.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F4.sf3.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Value-matrix fine-tuning</span></span>
<span id="S4.F4.sf3.5.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F4.sf3.5.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">with zero-shot loss</span></span>
</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.23197v1/linear_tf_both_v.svg" id="S4.F4.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/381;" width="476" height="381"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span>
<span id="S4.F4.sf4.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:310.5pt;">
<span id="S4.F4.sf4.5.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F4.sf4.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Value-matrix fine-tuning with</span></span>
<span id="S4.F4.sf4.5.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F4.sf4.5.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">zero- and few-shot losses</span></span>
</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<math id="S4.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-shot test errors on the target task <math id="S4.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for linear attention models under different training regimes.
Curves show theoretical predictions from Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, while points correspond to models trained empirically in Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Experiments on Linear Regression Tasks ‣ 5 Empirical Validation ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>. Empirical results match the theoretical values.
The parameters used for the theoretical predictions are given by:
<span id="S4.F4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(a) </span>(<a href="#S4.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ Corollary 4.1 (Optimal Parameters of Pretrained Models; Corollary of Theorem 1 in ( ) ). ‣ 4.1 Pretraining ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) from Corollary <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Corollary 4.1 (Optimal Parameters of Pretrained Models; Corollary of Theorem 1 in ( ) ). ‣ 4.1 Pretraining ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.
<span id="S4.F4.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b) </span>(<a href="#S4.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ Theorem 4.3 (Optimal Parameters of Fully Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.2 Fine-Tuning All Parameters with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) from Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3 (Optimal Parameters of Fully Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.2 Fine-Tuning All Parameters with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> with <math id="S4.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=0.52" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.52</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=0.52</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S4.F4.8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(c) </span>(<a href="#S4.E21" title="Equation 21 ‣ Theorem 4.6 (Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Value-Matrix Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) from Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 4.6 (Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Value-Matrix Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.6</span></a> with <math id="S4.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=0.77\;(=\tfrac{m}{m+1+d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.77</mn><mspace style="width:0.669em;" width="0.669em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=0.77\;(=\tfrac{m}{m+1+d})</annotation></semantics></math> (Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem7" title="Proposition 4.7. ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>).
<span id="S4.F4.8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(d) </span>(<a href="#S4.E21" title="Equation 21 ‣ Theorem 4.6 (Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Value-Matrix Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) with <math id="S4.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=0.66" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.66</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=0.66</annotation></semantics></math> (Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem9" title="Theorem 4.9 (Task-Wise Optimal 𝑤). ‣ 4.4 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot and Auxiliary Few-Shot Losses ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.9</span></a>; see Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.4 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot and Auxiliary Few-Shot Losses ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.9</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Task-Wise Optimal <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a target task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the family of parameters
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E21" title="Equation 21 ‣ Theorem 4.6 (Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Value-Matrix Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>).
For any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the few-shot test error on the task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> is minimized at</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w^{\star}(n;{\mathbf{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w^{\star}(n;{\mathbf{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\argmin_{w}\,\mathcal{E}\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\argmin_{w}\,\mathcal{E}\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}}\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{(n+1)\,{\mathbf{\theta}}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}-\frac{n+3+2d}{d+4}\,{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}}{(n+1+d)\,{\mathbf{\theta}}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}+d\sigma^{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{(n+1)\,{\mathbf{\theta}}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}-\frac{n+3+2d}{d+4}\,{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}}{(n+1+d)\,{\mathbf{\theta}}^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}{\mathbf{\theta}}+d\sigma^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\star}(n;{\mathbf{\theta}})\to\frac{d+3}{d+4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\star}(n;{\mathbf{\theta}})\to\frac{d+3}{d+4}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, for any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S4.E32.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:341.6pt;height:16pt;vertical-align:-4.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(43.1pt,-2.0pt) scale(1.33798479054724,1.33798479054724) ;">
<p id="S4.E32.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.E32.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{n\to\infty}\mathcal{E}\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(\tfrac{d+3}{d+4}),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}}\big)=\sigma^{2}+\frac{1}{(d+4)^{2}}\,({\mathbf{\theta}}-{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}({\mathbf{\theta}}-{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{n\to\infty}\mathcal{E}\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(\tfrac{d+3}{d+4}),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}}\big)=\sigma^{2}+\frac{1}{(d+4)^{2}}\,({\mathbf{\theta}}-{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})^{\top}{\mathbf{\Sigma}}({\mathbf{\theta}}-{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the target task <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem9" title="Theorem 4.9 (Task-Wise Optimal 𝑤). ‣ 4.4 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot and Auxiliary Few-Shot Losses ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.9</span></a> implies that the optimal value <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\star}(n;{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\star}(n;{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> increases with <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and converges to <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=\tfrac{d+3}{d+4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=\tfrac{d+3}{d+4}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. This result shows that the free parameter <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> can be tuned to adapt the fine-tuned model to a desired few-shot regime at test time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To control <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> during fine-tuning, we introduce an auxiliary few-shot prompt loss with <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> demonstrations. For the target task vector <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the few-shot prompt loss as</p>
<table id="S4.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FS}}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}):=\mathcal{E}\big({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\big)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>FS</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{FS}}({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}):=\mathcal{E}\big({\mathbf{V}},{\mathbf{Q}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">and fine-tune using a combination of the zero-shot loss (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Evaluation Metric and Training Objective. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) and the auxiliary few-shot loss.
Since the auxiliary loss depends on <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, it explicitly steers the learned parameter toward the value of <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> that optimizes <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-shot performance.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fine-Tuning with an Auxiliary Few-Shot Loss Improves Few-Shot Performance at the Expense of Generalization.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The task-wise optimal value <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\star}(n;{\mathbf{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\star}(n;{\mathbf{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E30" title="Equation 30 ‣ Theorem 4.9 (Task-Wise Optimal 𝑤). ‣ 4.4 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot and Auxiliary Few-Shot Losses ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a>) depends on the evaluation task <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As a result, choosing <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> to optimize few-shot performance on the target task <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> need not be optimal for other tasks <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}(\neq{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mspace style="width:0.389em;" width="0.389em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}(\neq{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. The following proposition quantifies this trade-off by characterizing the excess error incurred when the model uses <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\star}(n;{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\star}(n;{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> instead of the task-wise optimal choice <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\star}(n;{\mathbf{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\star}(n;{\mathbf{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.10</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a target task <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the family of parameters <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E21" title="Equation 21 ‣ Theorem 4.6 (Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Value-Matrix Fine-Tuned Models). ‣ 4.3 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot Loss ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>). Fix <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For simplicity, define <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}(w;\theta):=\mathcal{E}\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}(w;\theta):=\mathcal{E}\big(\hat{{\mathbf{V}}}(w),\hat{{\mathbf{Q}}};n,{\mathbf{\theta}}\big)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\star}(\theta):=w^{\star}(n;\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\star}(\theta):=w^{\star}(n;\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the excess error is</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{E}(w^{\star}(\theta_{0});\theta)-\mathcal{E}\big(w^{\star}(\theta);\theta\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{E}(w^{\star}(\theta_{0});\theta)-\mathcal{E}\big(w^{\star}(\theta);\theta\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{n\big((n\!+\!1\!+\!d)\theta^{\top}\Sigma\theta+d\sigma^{2}\big)}{(n\!+\!1)^{2}}\cdot\big(w^{\star}(\theta_{0})-w^{\star}(\theta)\big)^{2}\geq 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mpadded style="width:0.608em;" width="0.608em"><mo lspace="0.052em">+</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo rspace="0.052em">+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{n\big((n\!+\!1\!+\!d)\theta^{\top}\Sigma\theta+d\sigma^{2}\big)}{(n\!+\!1)^{2}}\cdot\big(w^{\star}(\theta_{0})-w^{\star}(\theta)\big)^{2}\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with equality if and only if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{\star}(\theta)=w^{\star}(\theta_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{\star}(\theta)=w^{\star}(\theta_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c:=\theta^{\top}\Sigma\theta=\theta_{0}^{\top}\Sigma\theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:=\theta^{\top}\Sigma\theta=\theta_{0}^{\top}\Sigma\theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, then (<a href="#S4.E34" title="Equation 34 ‣ Proposition 4.10. ‣ Fine-Tuning with an Auxiliary Few-Shot Loss Improves Few-Shot Performance at the Expense of Generalization. ‣ 4.4 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot and Auxiliary Few-Shot Losses ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>) reduces to</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{E}(w^{\star}(\theta_{0});\theta)-\mathcal{E}\big(w^{\star}(\theta);\theta\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{E}(w^{\star}(\theta_{0});\theta)-\mathcal{E}\big(w^{\star}(\theta);\theta\big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{n(n\!+\!3\!+\!2d)^{2}c^{2}}{(n\!+\!1)^{2}(d\!+\!4)^{2}\big((n\!+\!1\!+\!d)c+d\sigma^{2}\big)}\cdot\big(1\!-\!\rho(\theta,\theta_{0})\big)^{2}\!," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mpadded style="width:0.608em;" width="0.608em"><mo lspace="0.052em">+</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mpadded style="width:0.608em;" width="0.608em"><mo lspace="0.052em">+</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo rspace="0.052em">+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{n(n\!+\!3\!+\!2d)^{2}c^{2}}{(n\!+\!1)^{2}(d\!+\!4)^{2}\big((n\!+\!1\!+\!d)c+d\sigma^{2}\big)}\cdot\big(1\!-\!\rho(\theta,\theta_{0})\big)^{2}\!,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(\theta,\theta_{0})=\theta^{\top}\Sigma\theta_{0}/c\in[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(\theta,\theta_{0})=\theta^{\top}\Sigma\theta_{0}/c\in[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>
is the cosine similarity induced by the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>-inner product.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem10" title="Proposition 4.10. ‣ Fine-Tuning with an Auxiliary Few-Shot Loss Improves Few-Shot Performance at the Expense of Generalization. ‣ 4.4 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot and Auxiliary Few-Shot Losses ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.10</span></a> shows that tuning <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> using the auxiliary few-shot loss improves <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-shot performance on the target task <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, but can degrade performance on other tasks <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}(\neq{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mspace style="width:0.389em;" width="0.389em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}(\neq{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the degradation increases with the discrepancy between tasks, quantified by the cosine similarity <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho({\mathbf{\theta}},{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho({\mathbf{\theta}},{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> under the <math id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\Sigma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\Sigma}}</annotation></semantics></math>-inner product. This formalizes a fundamental limitation of auxiliary few-shot fine-tuning: while it enhances adaptation to the target task in the few-shot regime, it may reduce generalization across tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Appendix <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Proofs for Test Errors under the Auxiliary Few-Shot Prompt Loss ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>, we further analyze the test error when the zero-shot loss in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Evaluation Metric and Training Objective. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) is combined with the auxiliary few-shot loss in (<a href="#S4.E33" title="Equation 33 ‣ 4.4 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot and Auxiliary Few-Shot Losses ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>). In particular, Theorem <a href="#A1.Thmtheorem20" title="Theorem A.20. ‣ A.5 Proofs for Test Errors under the Auxiliary Few-Shot Prompt Loss ‣ Appendix A Proofs ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.20</span></a> shows that an additional gradient descent step on the few-shot loss increases the zero-shot test error once the parameters approach the optimal zero-shot solution. Motivated by this observation, we adopt an annealing strategy for the auxiliary few-shot loss in the following experimental section, gradually reducing its weight to zero during fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Empirical Validation</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we validate the theoretical results in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> through experiments on linear regression tasks. We further complement these findings with experiments on fine-tuning a large language model on the MMLU benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experiments on Linear Regression Tasks</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train the linear attention model in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Transformer Architecture. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) on linear regression tasks, where datasets are generated according to the setup in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Problem Setup ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahn et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, we set <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\Sigma}}={\mathbf{I}}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\Sigma}}={\mathbf{I}}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\|_{2}^{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}\|_{2}^{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n=20</annotation></semantics></math>. We first pretrain the model and then fine-tune it using three strategies described in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Further details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Experimental Setup and Additional Results for Linear Regression Tasks ‣ Appendix B Experimental Details ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.23197v1/linear_tf_thetas.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/271;" width="476" height="271"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<math id="S5.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-shot test errors evaluated on the in-distribution task <math id="S5.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S5.F5.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Left) </span>and the out-of-distribution task <math id="S5.F5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S5.F5.9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Right) </span>. Empty circles denote models fine-tuned with the zero-shot (ZS) loss only, whereas filled circles denote models fine-tuned with both the zero-shot and few-shot (FS) losses. Parentheses indicate which parameters are updated: <span id="S5.F5.9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">all</span> updates all parameters, <span id="S5.F5.9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Q</span> updates the query–key matrix, and <span id="S5.F5.9.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">V</span> updates the value matrix. Value-matrix fine-tuning without the auxiliary few-shot loss gives the lowest error on the out-of-distribution task <math id="S5.F5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.23197v1/mmlu.svg" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/313;" width="429" height="313"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F6.15" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S5.F6.16" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Zero-shot (x-axis) and few-shot (y-axis) accuracy of <span id="S5.F6.16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct</span> on the <em id="S5.F6.16.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Humanities</em> and <em id="S5.F6.16.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">STEM</em> categories of the MMLU benchmark.
We compare the pretrained model with three models fine-tuned to improve zero-shot performance on <em id="S5.F6.16.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Humanities</em>.
<span id="S5.F6.16.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(a) <span id="S5.F6.16.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">The pretrained model</span></span> exhibits higher few-shot than zero-shot accuracy on <em id="S5.F6.16.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Humanities</em>.
<span id="S5.F6.16.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(b) <span id="S5.F6.16.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">Fine-tuning with the zero-shot loss</span></span> improves zero-shot accuracy relative to the pretrained model but degrades few-shot accuracy.
<span id="S5.F6.16.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(c) <span id="S5.F6.16.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">Value-matrix fine-tuning with the zero-shot loss</span></span> preserves few-shot accuracy while achieving zero-shot accuracy comparable to <span id="S5.F6.16.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b) </span>.
<span id="S5.F6.16.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(d) <span id="S5.F6.16.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">Value-matrix fine-tuning with both zero-shot and few-shot losses</span></span> improves few-shot accuracy on <em id="S5.F6.16.11" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Humanities</em>, at the cost of reduced few-shot accuracy on other categories such as <em id="S5.F6.16.12" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">STEM</em>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.4 Fine-Tuning Value Matrix with Zero-Shot and Auxiliary Few-Shot Losses ‣ 4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> reports the test error on the target task <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, where points denote empirical results and curves show the theoretical predictions from Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Optimal Parameters and Test Error of Learned Linear Attention Models ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Across all settings, the empirical trends are consistent with the theoretical predictions.
Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.1 Experiments on Linear Regression Tasks ‣ 5 Empirical Validation ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> further reports test errors on the out-of-distribution task <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> under different fine-tuning strategies. Fine-tuning all parameters with both the zero-shot and auxiliary few-shot losses attains low error on <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, but exhibits higher few-shot error on <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast, value-matrix fine-tuning yields lower few-shot error on <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-{\mathbf{\theta}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating better retention of few-shot performance across tasks, an effect that is more pronounced without the auxiliary few-shot loss. In Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Experimental Setup and Additional Results for Linear Regression Tasks ‣ Appendix B Experimental Details ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>, we also consider models with separate query and key matrices, as well as multi-head architectures, which exhibit qualitatively similar trends.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Experiments on the MMLU Benchmark</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We complement our theoretical analysis with experiments on the MMLU benchmark using <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We fine-tune models to improve zero-shot accuracy on the <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Humanities</em> category and evaluate both zero-shot and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>-shot accuracy on <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Humanities</em> and <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">STEM</em>. We compare the pretrained model with three fine-tuning strategies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.1 Experiments on Linear Regression Tasks ‣ 5 Empirical Validation ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> illustrates trends consistent with our theoretical predictions. Full fine-tuning improves zero-shot accuracy on <em id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Humanities</em> but degrades few-shot accuracy. In contrast, value-matrix fine-tuning preserves few-shot performance while achieving comparable zero-shot accuracy. Incorporating an auxiliary few-shot loss further improves few-shot accuracy on <em id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Humanities</em>, but reduces few-shot accuracy on <em id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">STEM</em>. Additional results are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Experimental Setup and Additional Results for the MMLU Benchmark ‣ Appendix B Experimental Details ‣ Fine-Tuning Without Forgetting In-Context Learning:A Theoretical Analysis of Linear Attention Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate the effect of task-specific fine-tuning on the in-context learning capability of Transformers with linear attention.
Specifically, we derive closed-form expressions for the parameters that minimize the pretraining and fine-tuning objectives, which reveal how different fine-tuning regimes induce a distinct trade-off between zero-shot and few-shot performances.
Our analysis shows that fine-tuning all parameters to optimize the zero-shot performance can degrade the few-shot performance. In contrast, restricting updates to the value matrix preserves the few-shot performance while improving the zero-shot performance.
Furthermore, although incorporating an auxiliary few-shot loss enhances the performance on the target task, it may lead to degradation on out-of-distribution tasks.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations and Future Work.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our theoretical analysis relies on linear attention models, which offer analytical tractability and enable closed-form characterization of optimal parameters and test errors, but do not capture all architectural and optimization features of Transformer models. While experiments on the MMLU benchmark indicate that qualitatively similar phenomena may arise in large-scale models, these results should be interpreted as illustrative rather than conclusive. Extending the theory to more expressive attention mechanisms, such as softmax attention and multi-head architectures, and understanding how these insights generalize to broader parameter-efficient fine-tuning settings remain directions for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
